@BINd47 In the historical context, indentured servants were men and women who needed work and were having trouble finding it. So, they would contract with someone who might not have been able to pay them for their labor right away, but would be able to do so later on. They would work for that person and be provided room and board. In return, they worked on whatever tasks their master or mistress assigned them. Then they would be paid the full sum of what was agreed upon at the end of the contract.

At least that is the most basic concept. As I understand it, as it was practiced in pre-revolutionary America, it was just as binding as slavery and once signed, the servant couldn't leave unless the master/mistress was mistreating them or it could be proven that they wouldn't be able to pay the amount owed once the contract matured. An indentured servitude contract could also be sold, and thus the servant transferred to someone else.

That is just my understanding. I honestly haven't looked all that deep into the concept or its historical usage. I just know many prominent figures in 1700's America had them.

