@Dinzu uh, that's not how inbreeding works.

The problem with inbreeding is that if someone has a problematic recessive mutation, their offspring have a high chance of having it as well, such that if those offspring have a child there's a chance that grandchild will have two copies of the recessive gene.  The important term here is "problematic recessive mutation"... inbreeding doesn't create the mutation, it just has a much higher chance of getting two copies.  It takes many generations of inbreeding to concentrate natural mutations to the point where they cause viability problems beyond pre-existing bad genes.  Just having the same gene twice isn't, by itself, a problem... how do you think recessive traits like sickle cell and albinism work, given they involve having two copies of the same mutation?

So what happens if Jadis has zero problematic genes?  She's magically created by a god, seems odd that bad genes would be intentionally added when creating her.  Most likely she'd produce offspring that look extremely similar to her but otherwise completely healthy.

